  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 106 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nA 1006 mb surface low centered over northern Iowa early this \nafternoon will track slowly southeast toward central Illinois \ntonight, before continuing eastward into the Ohio Valley on \nSaturday. An arcing band of warm advection rainfall is currently \nspreading northeast across southwest Wisconsin. Lightning is \nrelatively sparse over the northern Mississippi Valley so far \ntoday, and radar returns have been weakening as they move closer \nto central Wisconsin. Another area of light rain is occurring over\nfar northern WI and the Upper Peninsula along a 700mb gradient. \nAs this low moves across the region, rain and thunderstorm trends \nremain the focus of this forecast.\n\nRainfall will continue to lift northeast and intensify through \nearly this evening as low-level warm advection strengthens \ndownstream of the Iowa low. While the overall boundary layer \nremains relatively stabilized by widespread cloud cover and \nearlier rain, isolated storms cannot be ruled out and most likely \nnear the U.P. border. The rain will likely continue over northeast\nWisconsin through Saturday morning before ending. With daytime\nheating, a few widely scattered showers could also develop in the\nafternoon from central to far northeast WI.  The highest \nprobabilities for 1 inch or greater of rainfall (30-50%) remain \nsituated south of a Marshfield to Green Bay line, with the highest\nconfidence in localized 1-inch amounts extending from Wautoma \ntoward Manitowoc. Highs on Saturday will be held in the 70s under \ncloud cover and persistent northeast winds.\n\nSurface high pressure will build into the Upper Great Lakes \nSaturday night and remain dominant through Monday night. This will\nyield clear to partly cloudy skies, light winds, and pleasant \nmid-summer conditions. High temperatures will steadily warm from \nthe mid and upper 70s on Sunday to the lower and mid 80s on Monday\nunder ample sunshine.\n\nThe next notable weather system arrives Tuesday into Wednesday as\nan upper-level shortwave trough and associated surface cold front\npush east across the Northern Plains into the Upper Midwest.\nMoisture return ahead of the boundary will push dew points back\ninto the 60s, generating MUCAPE of 500-1000 J/kg and deep-layer\nshear of 25-30 knots. This environment will bring scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms from Tuesday afternoon through Wednesday\nmorning. While severe weather risk remains low at this time due to\nmodest instability, parallel flow aloft relative to the frontal\nboundary could result in localized training of shower activity.\nLighter precipitation chances linger into Thursday as cooler,\nnear-normal air settles back over the region with highs returning\nto the upper 70s and lower 80s.",
